---
import Pillar from '../../layouts/Pillar.astro';
import ChildGrid from '../../components/ChildGrid.astro';
import { CHILDREN } from '../../data/clusters';
import { SITE } from '../../consts';

const items = CHILDREN['short-game'];
const schema = [{
  '@type': 'CollectionPage',
  name: 'Short Game: Where Amateur Golfers Lose the Most Strokes',
  description: 'Chipping, pitching, bunker play and putting. The scoring zone is where handicaps are actually made, and where practice pays back fastest.',
  hasPart: items.map((i) => ({
    '@type': 'WebPage', name: i.title, url: new URL(i.href, SITE.url).href,
  })),
}];
---
<Pillar
  cluster="short-game"
  title='Short Game: Where Amateur Golfers Lose the Most Strokes'
  description='Chipping, pitching, bunker play and putting. The scoring zone is where handicaps are actually made, and where practice pays back fastest.'
  intro='Inside 100 yards is the cheapest place in golf to find strokes. It needs no swing speed, no new equipment and no athletic ability — which is exactly why so few golfers practise it.'
  schema={schema}
>
  <div class="pil-prose">
      <h2>Why the short game pays back faster than anything else</h2>
      <p>
        A driver that goes fifteen yards further saves you a fraction of a stroke per round. Being
        able to get up and down instead of taking three to get down saves you a full stroke every
        time it happens, and for most mid-handicap golfers it happens several times a round.
      </p>
      <p>
        The short game also degrades least with age and requires the least physical capability. A
        68-year-old with a good short game will beat a strong 25-year-old without one, reliably,
        forever.
      </p>

      <h2>The honest reason it gets neglected</h2>
      <p>
        Range practice feels better. Hitting fifty drivers produces a handful of shots that feel
        superb, and that feeling is the reward. Hitting fifty chips produces no such moments — the
        good ones are unremarkable by definition. The practice that improves your score is
        frequently the practice that is least satisfying to do.
      </p>

      <div class="callout">
        <p class="callout-title">A useful test</p>
        <p>
          Drop ten balls around a practice green at varying lies and try to get each one up and
          down. Count the successes. Most golfers who describe their short game as "not bad" score
          two or three out of ten, and are genuinely surprised by it.
        </p>
      </div>
  </div>

  <ChildGrid items={items} heading="Guides in this section" />
</Pillar>

<style>
  .pil-prose { max-width: var(--w-prose); }
  .pil-prose :global(h2) { margin-top: var(--s-7); font-size: var(--fs-xl); }
  .pil-prose :global(li) { margin-bottom: var(--s-2); }
  .pil-prose :global(li::marker) { color: var(--brand); }
  .pil-prose :global(.table-scroll) { max-width: var(--w-content); }
</style>
